---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import { getPricingView } from '../../lib/pricing.ts';

const { manifest, model, anchors, providerPrice, ourPrice, margin } = await getPricingView();

const title = 'Why we price at cost + 10¢/hour — WhisperFree';
const description =
  'WhisperFree’s cloud models are billed at the provider’s cost plus a flat 10¢ per audio hour — and we publish the math. Here’s why.';
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} noindex={!manifest.enabled}>
  <Nav />
  <main class="pt-32 pb-20 px-6">
    <article class="max-w-2xl mx-auto">
      <p class="text-sm text-teal-700 font-semibold mb-3">Pricing</p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900 mb-6">
        Why we price at cost + 10&cent;/hour
      </h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-10">
        {manifest.enabled
          ? 'WhisperFree includes optional cloud models. We bill them at the provider’s cost plus a flat 10¢ per audio hour — and publish the whole calculation. Here’s the thinking.'
          : 'WhisperFree is preparing optional cloud models. At launch, we’ll bill them at the provider’s cost plus a flat 10¢ per audio hour — and publish the whole calculation. Here’s the thinking.'}
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-6 text-lg text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 pt-4">Local is still free. This is for the hard cases.</h2>
        <p>
          The local model runs entirely on your Mac, costs nothing, and is genuinely excellent in a
          quiet room. For most dictation, it&rsquo;s all you need &mdash; and it stays free.
        </p>
        <p>
          But a small on-device model struggles in exactly the places dictation gets hard:
          background noise, strong accents, dense jargon and names, and long rambling passages where
          errors compound. A frontier cloud model handles those much better.
          {manifest.enabled ? ' So we added one' : ' So we’re preparing one'} &mdash; optional, off
          by default, and yours to switch on only when you need it.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 pt-4">The whole business model, in one line</h2>
        <p>
          We charge what the provider charges us, plus {margin} an hour for the infrastructure that
          meters it. That&rsquo;s it. No subscription, no markup games, no &ldquo;contact
          sales.&rdquo;
        </p>
        <p>
          At launch the one model is {model.label}: the provider&rsquo;s price is about
          {' '}{providerPrice} per audio hour, we add {margin}, and you pay <strong>{ourPrice} per audio
          hour</strong>. The same pricing manifest the backend publishes is what renders our
          {' '}
          <a href="/pricing" class="text-teal-700 underline">pricing page</a> &mdash; so the
          published numbers come from the same versioned pricing manifest the app uses. If that
          manifest cannot be verified, the site treats cloud models as unavailable.
        </p>
        <p>
          Transparency cuts both ways: providers have billing quirks, and those pass through to you
          rather than getting hidden in a round number. We&rsquo;d rather show the seams than pretend
          there aren&rsquo;t any.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 pt-4">Why per-second, not per-month</h2>
        <p>
          Most people dictate far less than they assume &mdash; minutes a day, not hours. An audio
          hour is a <em>lot</em> of talking, and it costs cents. A heavy day of dictation is around
          15 minutes of audio: roughly <strong>{anchors.perFifteenMinCents}&cent;</strong>.
          {' '}
          <strong>$5</strong> of credit lasts roughly
          {' '}<strong>{anchors.hoursPerFiveDollars} hours</strong> &mdash; about
          {' '}
          {anchors.monthsPerFiveDollarsAtOneHourPerWeek} months if you dictate an hour a week.
        </p>
        <p>
          You are billed only for completed cloud transcription. Add credit when you want it;
          purchased credit does not expire, and there is no recurring subscription.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 pt-4">The privacy tradeoff, stated plainly</h2>
        <p>
          Cloud transcription sends only the clips you choose through WhisperFree&rsquo;s backend to
          OpenAI. WhisperFree holds audio in request memory and does not persist audio or
          transcripts. As checked on October 17, 2026, OpenAI lists its audio-transcription endpoint
          as having no abuse-monitoring or application-state retention, and says API data is not
          used for training unless the customer opts in. Provider policies can change, so the
          {' '}
          <a href="https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/your-data" class="text-teal-700 underline" rel="noopener">live data-controls page</a>
          remains the source of truth. Local mode avoids the provider entirely.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 pt-4">Why bother being this open?</h2>
        <p>
          Because the brand was built on trust, and transparent pricing is the honest version of
          that for a paid feature. The 10&cent; buys zero setup, accurate metering, and one-click
          switching &mdash; and the confidence that we&rsquo;re not quietly marking anything up.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 pt-8 border-t border-gray-100">
        <a href="/pricing" class="text-teal-700 underline font-medium">See the full pricing breakdown &rarr;</a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
